import{_ as o,o as t,c as d,ag as a}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Adapters","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"components/adapters.md","filePath":"components/adapters.md"}'),s={name:"components/adapters.md"};function c(n,e,i,r,l,h){return t(),d("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="adapters" tabindex="-1">Adapters <a class="header-anchor" href="#adapters" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Adapters&quot;">​</a></h1><p>An adapter is the part of the harness that belongs to one frontend. The neutral sources in <code>agents/</code>, <code>skills/</code>, <code>rules/</code>, <code>instructions/</code> and <code>commands/</code> name no frontend. Each adapter turns them into the configuration of its frontend, and adds the files that only its frontend reads. The sources of an adapter are below <code>adapters/&lt;frontend&gt;/</code>: <code>adapters/claude/</code> for Claude Code, <code>adapters/opencode/</code> for OpenCode, <code>adapters/omp/</code> for oh-my-pi and <code>adapters/antigravity/</code> for Antigravity.</p><p>Each adapter has one Python module, <code>adapter.py</code>. <code>lib/harness/frontends.py</code> loads the four modules by path, in the order Claude Code, OpenCode, oh-my-pi, Antigravity. Each module gives its own subcommands, its part of <code>harness install</code>, its test cases for <code>harness test</code>, and the restart line that the install prints after a change. The other files of an adapter are instruction text, configuration and extension code. <code>harness install --apply</code> copies or renders them into the configuration directory of the frontend. <a href="./../architecture#the-opencode-and-oh-my-pi-adapters">The architecture</a> describes what each frontend receives, and <a href="./harness">the harness command</a> describes the loader.</p><p>The sections follow the four frontends: Claude Code, OpenCode, oh-my-pi, then Antigravity. In each group, the module <code>adapter.py</code> comes first, then the files that it installs.</p><h2 id="claude-adapter-py" tabindex="-1"><code>claude/adapter.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#claude-adapter-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`claude/adapter.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The adapter of Claude Code. It writes the Claude Code layer of <code>harness install</code>, and it gives the subcommands <code>harness settings</code> and <code>harness trust</code>.</p><p>The layer goes into <code>~/.claude/</code>. The five neutral directories install at their own path, <code>adapters/claude/</code> at the top, <code>hooks/</code> into <code>~/.claude/hooks/</code>, and the tree instructions at their own path. An agent and a <code>SKILL.md</code> get two values rewritten: the model tier of <code>model:</code> becomes the model of the <code>[claude]</code> table of <code>models.toml</code>, and each <code>tools:</code> item its Claude Code name. A hook keeps its mode, and every other file is installed with mode 0644.</p><p>The first file of the layer is <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>. The adapter merges four sections of the settings template into this file: <code>permissions</code>, <code>hooks</code>, <code>sandbox</code> and <code>modelSettings</code>. It adds each other key of the template, and each <code>env</code> variable, that the file does not have. It keeps all other keys and values, keeps the old file as a backup, and creates the file if it is missing. The dry run prints the diff of those sections after the line of the file. After the layer, the adapter writes the stamp <code>~/.claude/.harness-install.json</code>.</p><p>The adapter exits with status 2, before it writes a file, in these cases:</p><ul><li>two sources hold the same path;</li><li>a source or an installed path is a symbolic link;</li><li>a source holds <code>settings.json</code>;</li><li><code>settings.json</code> is not a JSON object, or one of the four sections in it is not an object.</li></ul><p>An installed file with no source is an EXTRA line with its removal command. The permission rules of the other settings files of the research tree are a warning.</p><p><code>harness settings</code> has four subcommands: <code>surface</code>, <code>compare</code>, <code>selftest</code> and <code>twins</code>. <code>surface</code> reads the session transcripts below <code>~/.claude/projects/</code> and counts the Bash commands that reach a prompt. <code>harness trust</code> marks the research root and each git repository below it as trusted in <code>~/.claude.json</code>. It skips <code>.worktrees/</code> and <code>.scratch/</code>. A session cannot read that file, so run it in your own terminal.</p><p><code>fixtures/</code> holds the expected rendered output of two agents for Claude Code, which the cases compare.</p><h2 id="claude-claude-md" tabindex="-1"><code>claude/CLAUDE.md</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#claude-claude-md" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`claude/CLAUDE.md`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The global instruction file of Claude Code. <code>harness install</code> copies it to <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>, and every Claude Code session reads it at the start.</p><p>Its first lines import <code>RTK.md</code>, <code>instructions/core.md</code> and <code>instructions/research-tree.md</code>, the last from the tree instructions. The rest holds the mechanics of Claude Code only:</p><ul><li>the worktrees, which the <code>WorktreeCreate</code> hook makes, and <code>EnterWorktree</code> and <code>ExitWorktree</code>;</li><li>the trust of a new clone, which needs <code>harness trust --apply</code> from the user;</li><li>a workflow for fan-out over many repositories;</li><li>the wait for a long command: run the command that gives the answer in the background, never a <code>sleep</code>;</li><li><code>git</code> and <code>gh</code> as the whole command, so that they run outside the Claude Code sandbox;</li><li>the sandbox as the security boundary, and the deny list as protection against accidents.</li></ul><p>OpenCode, oh-my-pi and Antigravity do not read this file. They read <code>opencode/OPENCODE-DELTA.md</code>, <code>omp/OMP-DELTA.md</code> and <code>antigravity/ANTIGRAVITY-DELTA.md</code> in its place. A rule for every frontend goes into <code>instructions/core.md</code>, not here.</p><h2 id="claude-rtk-md" tabindex="-1"><code>claude/RTK.md</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#claude-rtk-md" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`claude/RTK.md`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The facts about RTK, a proxy that rewrites shell commands so that their output uses fewer tokens. Under Claude Code, the <code>PreToolUse</code> hook <code>rtk hook claude</code> does the rewrite. <code>harness install</code> copies the file to <code>~/.claude/RTK.md</code>.</p><p>The file tells the agent:</p><ul><li><code>rtk run</code>, <code>rtk proxy</code>, <code>rtk curl</code> and <code>rtk wget</code> are denied;</li><li><code>rtk hook check &#39;&lt;cmd&gt;&#39;</code> shows whether a command is rewritten;</li><li>in Claude Code, a pipeline or a redirection turns the rewrite off for the whole command;</li><li><code>head</code> and <code>tail</code> are not rewritten;</li><li><code>rtk grep</code> and <code>rtk rg</code> stop at a limit per file, so read the first and the last lines;</li><li>use the <code>Read</code> tool when the exact bytes matter.</li></ul><p>Four frontends read it, each its own copy. <code>CLAUDE.md</code> imports <code>~/.claude/RTK.md</code>. OpenCode lists its copy in the <code>instructions</code> of <code>opencode.jsonc</code>. <code>OMP-DELTA.md</code> imports oh-my-pi&#39;s copy, but oh-my-pi runs no rewrite. Antigravity loads its copy, <code>~/.gemini/config/rules/RTK.md</code>, as a rule with <code>trigger: always_on</code>, and runs no rewrite either.</p><h2 id="claude-statusline-command-sh" tabindex="-1"><code>claude/statusline-command.sh</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#claude-statusline-command-sh" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`claude/statusline-command.sh`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The status line of Claude Code. The script reads the session data, as JSON, from its standard input with <code>jq</code>. It prints one line: the model, the effort, the input and output tokens, and the use of the five-hour and the weekly rate limits. A use below 50 % is green, below 80 % yellow, and from 80 % red. The five-hour limit also shows the time until it resets.</p><p><code>harness install</code> copies the script to <code>~/.claude/statusline-command.sh</code>, with mode 0644. The settings template does not name the script. Point the status line setting of Claude Code at the installed copy.</p><p>The script uses POSIX <code>sh</code>, <code>jq</code> and <code>date +%s</code> only, so it runs on macOS and Linux. Without <code>jq</code> the line shows no values.</p><h2 id="opencode-adapter-py" tabindex="-1"><code>opencode/adapter.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#opencode-adapter-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`opencode/adapter.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The adapter of OpenCode. It writes OpenCode&#39;s part of <code>harness install</code>, and it gives the subcommand <code>harness permissions</code>.</p><p>The install writes into <code>$OPENCODE_CONFIG_DIR</code>, or <code>~/.config/opencode</code> if it is not set. The directory must exist. The adapter writes:</p><ul><li><code>opencode.jsonc</code>, rendered with the profile, with a backup of a file that it replaces;</li><li><code>AGENTS.md</code>, from <code>OPENCODE-DELTA.md</code>;</li><li><code>agents/&lt;name&gt;.md</code> for each agent of <code>agents/</code>;</li><li>the plugins <code>plugins/*.ts</code> and the rendered <code>plugins/guard-paths.json</code>;</li><li>its own copies of the files of the Claude Code layer that OpenCode reads: <code>RTK.md</code>, <code>instructions/</code>, <code>rules/</code>, the guard hooks in <code>hooks/</code> and the skills in <code>skills/</code>.</li></ul><p>In each text except <code>AGENTS.md</code>, a path below <code>~/.claude/</code> names the same path in OpenCode&#39;s directory, so OpenCode reads its own copies. The install also removes the links in <code>~/.agents/skills/</code> that an earlier install wrote, because OpenCode reads that directory too.</p><p>The port of an agent maps the model tier through the model tables of <code>models.toml</code>, which OpenCode shares with oh-my-pi, with the keys of <code>[opencode]</code> over them. It turns <code>tools:</code> and <code>skills:</code> into a <code>permission:</code> block, and adds an &quot;Under OpenCode&quot; section before the body. A council is a set of copies of an agent on other models, and a seat is one of these copies. The description of a seat with <code>verify = true</code> says that it also judges each verify round, a later critic round that checks only the fix. The install refuses to replace an <code>opencode.jsonc</code> that holds a literal <code>Authorization</code> value, unless you give <code>--force</code>.</p><p><code>harness permissions [--apply]</code> writes the permission block of <code>opencode.jsonc</code> and <code>plugins/guard-paths.json</code> from the <code>ask</code> and <code>deny</code> rules of the settings template. Its report shows that every rule is emitted or dropped with a reason.</p><p><code>fixtures/</code> holds the input agents, their expected rendered output, a settings file, and the expected permission block, path list and report. The cases compare the output with them.</p><h2 id="opencode-opencode-jsonc" tabindex="-1"><code>opencode/opencode.jsonc</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#opencode-opencode-jsonc" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`opencode/opencode.jsonc`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The configuration template of OpenCode. <code>harness install</code> renders it with the profile and writes it to <code>opencode.jsonc</code> in OpenCode&#39;s configuration directory.</p><p>It sets the default model and the model for background work. Its <code>instructions</code> list <code>RTK.md</code>, <code>instructions/core.md</code> and <code>instructions/research-tree.md</code>, because OpenCode does not follow a file reference in an instruction file. The template names them below <code>~/.claude/</code>, and the install writes the paths of OpenCode&#39;s own copies. The <code>provider</code> entries come from <code>providers.toml</code> beside the profile, as <a href="./../profile#the-providers">profile.md</a> maps them. Before them, the install writes one entry for each provider of the <code>context_limits</code> of <code>models.toml</code>, with that limit. When a provider is in both, the install stops with exit status 2. The <code>mcp</code> entry connects to the Kaimon server at <code>http://127.0.0.1:2828/</code>, which <code>launchagents/kaimon.plist</code> starts. The token comes from <code>~/.config/kaimon/auth-token</code> when OpenCode reads the file, so no secret is in the repository. <code>experimental.subagent_depth</code> is 2, so that a builder that the dispatcher starts can start <code>julia-branch-verifier</code> or a <code>reader</code>. The dispatcher is the session that runs the <code>build-part</code> or <code>build-reviewed</code> skill. OpenCode allows one level by default.</p><p>The <code>permission</code> block is generated. Do not edit it by hand: run <code>harness permissions --apply</code>, which writes it from the settings template. OpenCode takes the last rule that matches, so each <code>ask</code> comes before each <code>deny</code>. The <code>allow</code> rules of the template are not ported. OpenCode matches a path pattern against the path relative to the project root, so each <code>**/X</code> read rule is followed by its root form <code>X</code>, which <code>plugins/guard-paths.json</code> leaves out. <code>external_directory</code> starts with <code>&quot;*&quot;: &quot;deny&quot;</code>: OpenCode&#39;s built-in rules allow every path, and a file tool is refused outside the allow entries that follow.</p><p>OpenCode has no sandbox. This permission block is its only control, and a command inside a string argument escapes it.</p><h2 id="opencode-opencode-delta-md" tabindex="-1"><code>opencode/OPENCODE-DELTA.md</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#opencode-opencode-delta-md" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`opencode/OPENCODE-DELTA.md`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The global instruction file of OpenCode. <code>harness install</code> writes it as <code>AGENTS.md</code> in OpenCode&#39;s configuration directory. There it takes the place of <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>. The core rules, <code>RTK.md</code> and the tree instructions load beside it through <code>opencode.jsonc</code>.</p><p>It states the facts of OpenCode:</p><ul><li>which rule file to read before which kind of work, because OpenCode loads no rule by itself;</li><li>there is no sandbox, and <code>external_directory</code> is the one boundary;</li><li>pass <code>workdir</code> and absolute paths, and do not <code>cd</code> inside a command;</li><li>there is no session scratchpad, no worktree hook and no background run;</li><li>the plugins <code>rtk.ts</code> and <code>guards.ts</code> take the place of the hooks;</li><li><code>opencode --auto</code> removes every prompt, so do not use it to push, merge or release;</li><li>how to call the agents, and the council of critics of <code>build-part</code>.</li></ul><p>The file loads into every session, so keep it small. <code>opencode.jsonc</code> gives its budget as 5,120 bytes.</p><h2 id="opencode-plugins-env-ts" tabindex="-1"><code>opencode/plugins/env.ts</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#opencode-plugins-env-ts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`opencode/plugins/env.ts`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An OpenCode plugin that sets environment variables for every shell command. OpenCode has no <code>env</code> key in its configuration, so the plugin sets the variables in the hook <code>create.before</code> of the shell.</p><p>It sets the variables of the <code>env</code> of the Claude Code settings:</p><ul><li><code>JULIA_PKG_USE_CLI_GIT=true</code>: Pkg uses the <code>git</code> program and not libgit2.</li><li><code>JULIA_NUM_THREADS=2</code> and <code>OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=2</code>: a part runs up to three critics and a suite at the same time, and without a limit each Julia process starts one BLAS thread per core.</li></ul><p>It does not port <code>CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS</code>, which has no meaning for OpenCode.</p><p><code>harness install</code> copies the plugin to <code>plugins/env.ts</code> in OpenCode&#39;s configuration directory. The hook probe <code>hooks/probe.py</code> loads it under <code>node</code>, so it holds JavaScript syntax only.</p><h2 id="opencode-plugins-guards-ts" tabindex="-1"><code>opencode/plugins/guards.ts</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#opencode-plugins-guards-ts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`opencode/plugins/guards.ts`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The OpenCode plugin of the guard hooks. It runs the install&#39;s copies in <code>hooks/</code> of OpenCode&#39;s configuration directory. On every <code>shell</code> call it runs the five shell guards, <code>no-blind-stage.py</code>, <code>no-shell-file-write.py</code>, <code>gh-api-writes.py</code>, <code>rm-scope.py</code> and <code>git-archive-output.py</code>, and the working-tree check of <code>sandbox-semantics.py</code>, as <code>sandbox-semantics.py --work-tree</code>. On every <code>kaimon_ex</code> and <code>kaimon_manage_repl</code> call it runs <code>kaimon-session.py</code>. It sends Claude Code&#39;s payload on standard input and reads the answer as Claude Code does. <a href="./hooks">Guard hooks</a> describes each script.</p><p>The plugin adds two checks. The path guard refuses a command that names a path of <code>guard-paths.json</code> beside the plugin, and of <code>$OPENCODE_CONFIG_DIR/guard-paths.json</code> when it exists. It also refuses a directory that holds a denied path when the command recurses, globs or changes into it. A second check refuses an in-place edit, such as <code>sed -i</code>, with a short message.</p><p>The plugin fails closed. A script that cannot start, fails or times out after 10 s refuses the command. A missing path list refuses every <code>shell</code> call. So does a <code>$OPENCODE_CONFIG_DIR/guard-paths.json</code> that is present and cannot be read or is not JSON. A guard that would ask refuses too, and the model must ask the user in chat.</p><p>The path guard checks text only. A path that a command builds at run time, or a relative path, passes it.</p><h2 id="opencode-plugins-guard-paths-json" tabindex="-1"><code>opencode/plugins/guard-paths.json</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#opencode-plugins-guard-paths-json" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`opencode/plugins/guard-paths.json`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The deny list of the path guard of <code>guards.ts</code>. It holds the <code>read</code> denies of OpenCode&#39;s permission block: the <code>Read</code> denies of the settings template, and one deny that only OpenCode needs, its own data directory.</p><p><code>harness permissions --apply</code> writes the file from the settings template. Do not edit it by hand. The paths hold <code>{home}</code>, so <code>harness install</code> renders the file with the profile and writes it to <code>plugins/guard-paths.json</code> in OpenCode&#39;s configuration directory.</p><p>A <code>read</code> deny binds only the file tools of OpenCode. With this list, <code>guards.ts</code> refuses a shell command that names the same path, for example a <code>cat</code> of a credential file.</p><p>The oh-my-pi adapter reads the list too. Its <code>deny</code> becomes the <code>deny</code> of oh-my-pi&#39;s path list.</p><h2 id="opencode-plugins-rtk-ts" tabindex="-1"><code>opencode/plugins/rtk.ts</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#opencode-plugins-rtk-ts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`opencode/plugins/rtk.ts`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The OpenCode plugin of RTK. RTK has no hook of its own for OpenCode, so this plugin does the rewrite. On each <code>shell</code> call it runs <code>rtk hook check &lt;command&gt;</code>. Exit 0 gives the rewritten command, which replaces the command of the call.</p><p>The plugin never fails a command. When RTK is absent, fails or takes more than 10 s, the command runs as it is. It calls <code>rtk</code> from the <code>PATH</code> of OpenCode, as the hook of Claude Code does. <code>harness install</code> warns when no <code>rtk</code> is on the <code>PATH</code>; the warning does not change its exit status.</p><p>The rewrite differs from the hook of Claude Code: the plugin also rewrites the first command of a pipeline. So <code>grep -n p f | cat</code> stops at the limit of <code>rtk grep</code>. For a complete search, use the <code>grep</code> tool.</p><p><code>harness install</code> copies the plugin to <code>plugins/rtk.ts</code>. OpenCode runs the plugins in the order of their names, so <code>guards.ts</code> checks a command before <code>rtk.ts</code> rewrites it.</p><h2 id="omp-adapter-py" tabindex="-1"><code>omp/adapter.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#omp-adapter-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`omp/adapter.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The adapter of oh-my-pi. It writes oh-my-pi&#39;s part of <code>harness install</code> and gives no subcommand of its own.</p><p>The install writes into oh-my-pi&#39;s agent directory, <code>$PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR</code>, or <code>~/.omp/agent</code> if it is not set:</p><ul><li><code>harness.yml</code>, an overlay over oh-my-pi&#39;s own <code>config.yml</code>, which the install never writes (see below);</li><li><code>modelRoles.default</code> in <code>harness.yml</code>, the <code>medium</code> model of the model tables, and an empty <code>retry.fallbackChains</code> entry for each model of an agent or a council seat, so that a provider error does not move an agent to another model. A model whose chain in <code>config.yml</code> names only the same model through another provider keeps that chain;</li><li><code>models.yml</code>, after a backup: the providers of <code>providers.toml</code> beside the profile, and each model of the <code>context_limits</code> of <code>models.toml</code> with its limit as <code>contextWindow</code>. The install stops with exit status 2 when a limit is on a provider of <code>providers.toml</code>;</li><li><code>extensions/guards.ts</code> and the path list <code>extensions/guard-paths.json</code>;</li><li><code>AGENTS.md</code>, from <code>OMP-DELTA.md</code>;</li><li><code>rules/&lt;name&gt;.md</code> for each rule that the Claude Code layer installs, with <code>paths</code> written as <code>globs</code>;</li><li><code>mcp.json</code>, with the Kaimon server alone and a request timeout of 120 s, above Kaimon&#39;s own move of a slow eval to the background;</li><li><code>agents/&lt;name&gt;.md</code> for each agent of <code>agents/</code>, with its model and its <code>thinking-level</code> from the model tables and the section of <code>UNDER-OMP.md</code>, and a copy for each seat of its council;</li><li>its own copies of <code>RTK.md</code>, <code>instructions/</code>, the guard hooks in <code>hooks/</code> and the skills in <code>skills/</code>. In each text except <code>AGENTS.md</code>, a path below <code>~/.claude/</code> names the same path in oh-my-pi&#39;s directory.</li></ul><p><code>harness.yml</code> sets the approval mode <code>yolo</code> and denies the <code>eval</code> tool. It writes the Bash rules of the settings template as <code>bash.patterns</code>: every <code>deny</code>, then every <code>ask</code> as <code>prompt</code>, then every <code>allow</code>, then a <code>prompt</code> for <code>gh pr create*</code>, which <code>yolo</code> would otherwise run without a prompt when it names no repository. oh-my-pi reads the file when <code>PI_CONFIG_FILES</code> in <code>~/.omp/agent/.env</code> names it, and the install warns until it does. oh-my-pi has no <code>env</code> setting either, so the install also warns, with the lines, until the <code>.env</code> or the environment sets each variable of the template&#39;s <code>env</code> except the <code>CLAUDE_CODE_*</code> variables.</p><p>oh-my-pi takes the first pattern that matches, so this order keeps the precedence of Claude Code. The model tables are the ones that OpenCode reads, with the keys of <code>[omp]</code> over them, so the agents of both frontends run on the same models. The install exits with status 2 when a rule source is outside the grammar of <code>render_rule</code> or in a subdirectory of <code>rules/</code>, and when the table <code>TOOLS</code> does not map a tool. An installed rule, agent or skill with no source is an EXTRA line.</p><p>The adapter has no <code>fixtures/</code> directory. Its cases run the install on a scratch <code>HOME</code>.</p><h2 id="omp-omp-delta-md" tabindex="-1"><code>omp/OMP-DELTA.md</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#omp-omp-delta-md" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`omp/OMP-DELTA.md`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The global instruction file of oh-my-pi. <code>harness install</code> writes it as <code>AGENTS.md</code> in oh-my-pi&#39;s agent directory, byte for byte. Its first three lines import oh-my-pi&#39;s copies of <code>RTK.md</code>, <code>instructions/core.md</code> and <code>instructions/research-tree.md</code>, below <code>~/.omp/agent/</code>.</p><p>It states the facts of oh-my-pi:</p><ul><li>there is no sandbox, and <code>bash.patterns</code> and the guard extension are the two controls;</li><li>oh-my-pi runs no RTK rewrite, so a command runs as typed;</li><li>the approval mode is <code>yolo</code>, and the <code>eval</code> tool is denied;</li><li>what the guard extension refuses;</li><li>there is no session scratchpad, no worktree hook and no background run;</li><li>read a <code>CLAUDE.md</code> below the working directory yourself;</li><li>read a rule through <code>rule://&lt;name&gt;</code> before work on a file that its globs match;</li><li>the skills are oh-my-pi&#39;s copies in <code>~/.omp/agent/skills/</code>, read through <code>skill://&lt;name&gt;</code>;</li><li>each agent&#39;s model is in its frontmatter, and a council seat needs its provider&#39;s credentials;</li><li>the dispatcher continues an agent by starting a new one, never by a write to <code>agent://</code>, and runs a command that a sub-agent returns for approval;</li><li>the council of critics of <code>build-part</code>.</li></ul><p>The cases check that the file is no larger than <code>OPENCODE-DELTA.md</code>.</p><h2 id="omp-under-omp-md" tabindex="-1"><code>omp/UNDER-OMP.md</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#omp-under-omp-md" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`omp/UNDER-OMP.md`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The text of the &quot;Under oh-my-pi&quot; section that the oh-my-pi adapter puts before the body of an agent. The section replaces each Claude Code mechanism that the agent uses.</p><p>The file has one level-2 section for each mechanism: <code>intro</code>, <code>skills</code>, <code>worktree</code>, <code>agent</code>, <code>bash</code>, <code>write</code>, <code>pr</code> and <code>mcp</code>. <code>render_agent</code> takes <code>intro</code>, then the section of each mechanism that the agent uses: preloaded skills, worktree isolation, the <code>Agent</code> tool, the shell tool, the write tool, a body that opens the pull request, or a Kaimon tool. <code>bash</code> has a sub-agent return a command that needs approval to its caller; <code>write</code> forbids a write to another agent; <code>pr</code> has the builder return <code>gh pr create</code> to its caller. In <code>skills</code>, <code>{skills}</code> becomes the names of the agent&#39;s skills. An agent that uses none of these mechanisms gets no section.</p><p>Edit this file to change what every ported agent reads under oh-my-pi. When a section that the adapter needs is missing, the install exits with status 2.</p><h2 id="omp-guards-ts" tabindex="-1"><code>omp/guards.ts</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#omp-guards-ts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`omp/guards.ts`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The guard extension of oh-my-pi. <code>harness install</code> copies it to <code>extensions/guards.ts</code> in oh-my-pi&#39;s agent directory. It checks each tool call that a glob of <code>bash.patterns</code> cannot check:</p><ul><li><code>bash</code>: the five shell guards of <code>~/.omp/agent/hooks/</code> and the working-tree check of <code>sandbox-semantics.py</code>, which it runs with <code>python3</code>, and the path list on the words of the command and on its working directory;</li><li><code>mcp__kaimon_ex</code> and <code>mcp__kaimon_manage_repl</code>: <code>kaimon-session.py</code>;</li><li><code>read</code> and <code>grep</code>: the <code>deny</code> list on the path;</li><li><code>edit</code> and <code>write</code>: the <code>edit</code> list, then the <code>ask</code> list, on each path that the call names;</li><li><code>write</code> in a sub-agent: a path <code>agent://&lt;name&gt;</code>, which it refuses, because the write wakes a parked agent. oh-my-pi gives a sub-agent&#39;s context <code>agent.kind: &quot;sub&quot;</code>.</li></ul><p>A word with a glob character reaches only the names that its first glob segment matches, so a pattern such as <code>/[0-9]+/</code> reaches no denied path. The program argument of an awk, sed or grep is not checked as a path; a home path in it is still refused.</p><p>The path list is <code>guard-paths.json</code> beside the extension. The <code>deny</code> and <code>edit</code> lists also get <code>~/.omp/**</code>. The copies of the skills, the rules and the instructions below <code>~/.omp/agent/</code> stay readable. The match ignores case. An <code>ask</code> match and an &quot;ask&quot; answer of a guard refuse the call, and tell the model to ask the user in chat.</p><p>The extension fails closed: a guard that cannot run, fails or times out blocks the call, and so does a missing path list. It checks text only. A path that a command builds at run time passes, and the <code>find</code> and <code>ast_grep</code> tools are not checked.</p><h2 id="antigravity-adapter-py" tabindex="-1"><code>antigravity/adapter.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#antigravity-adapter-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`antigravity/adapter.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The adapter of Antigravity. It writes Antigravity&#39;s part of <code>harness install</code> and gives no subcommand of its own.</p><p>The install writes below <code>~/.gemini/</code>, once Antigravity has made that directory; without it, the plan holds no file and one warning. The desktop app, the IDE and the CLI <code>agy</code> read the one directory <code>~/.gemini/config/</code>. The adapter writes:</p><ul><li><code>config/hooks.json</code>, with one <code>SessionStart</code> handler, which runs <code>install-drift.py</code> and gives its warning to the session, and one <code>PreToolUse</code> group with the matcher <code>*</code>, which runs <code>guard.py</code> by its absolute path on every tool call;</li><li><code>guard.py</code>, the seven guard scripts and <code>install-drift.py</code> in <code>config/hooks/</code>, and the path list <code>config/guard-paths.json</code>;</li><li><code>config/GEMINI.md</code>, from <code>ANTIGRAVITY-DELTA.md</code>;</li><li><code>config/rules/&lt;name&gt;.md</code>: <code>RTK.md</code> and each file of <code>instructions/</code> as a rule with <code>trigger: always_on</code>, and each rule of <code>rules/</code> with its <code>paths</code> as <code>trigger: glob</code>;</li><li><code>config/skills/&lt;name&gt;/</code>, the skills, each <code>SKILL.md</code> without its <code>model:</code>;</li><li><code>config/agents/&lt;name&gt;.md</code> for each agent of <code>agents/</code>, with its model from the table <code>[antigravity]</code> of <code>models.toml</code>, its tools from the table <code>TOOLS</code>, and the section of <code>UNDER-ANTIGRAVITY.md</code>;</li><li><code>config/mcp_config.json</code>, with mode 600 and no backup: the file as it is, with the entry <code>kaimon</code> set to Kaimon&#39;s URL and its token from <code>~/.config/kaimon/auth-token</code> written out, because Antigravity expands no shell form in a header. With no token, the install leaves the file as it is and gives one warning;</li><li>three keys of the CLI&#39;s <code>antigravity-cli/settings.json</code>: <code>permissions</code>, <code>toolPermission</code> and <code>enableTerminalSandbox</code>. The merge keeps every other key of that file and its indent, and backs up the file it replaces;</li><li>the same permission rules in the desktop app&#39;s grants, <code>userSettings.globalPermissionGrants</code> of <code>config/config.json</code>: each rule once in its list, <code>allow</code>, <code>deny</code> or <code>ask</code>, after the entries there. The merge keeps every other key and every grant of the user in its place, removes no entry, and backs up the file it replaces. The app&#39;s grants per project, in <code>config/projects/</code>, stay as they are.</li></ul><p>In each text except <code>GEMINI.md</code>, a path below <code>~/.claude/</code> names Antigravity&#39;s copy, and <code>RTK.md</code> and the files of <code>instructions/</code> are in <code>config/rules/</code>. Antigravity loads no glob rule of <code>config/rules/</code>, so the delta lists each rule with the files it applies to.</p><p>The permission rules are the settings template&#39;s: a Bash entry becomes a <code>command(…)</code> rule, a Read or Edit path a <code>read_file(…)</code> or <code>write_file(…)</code> rule, and an allow host of the network lists a <code>read_url(…)</code> rule. A <code>*</code> of an allow rule in regex form matches no text that ends a segment, so the rule never matches across <code>;</code>, <code>|</code>, <code>&amp;</code>, a substitution or a redirection. A path with a glob that Antigravity cannot express is dropped, and the path lists of <code>guard.py</code> cover it. Each Kaimon tool of the template, <code>mcp__kaimon__&lt;tool&gt;</code>, becomes <code>mcp(kaimon/&lt;tool&gt;)</code> after the other rules of its list, in <code>deny</code> before the rules of <code>denyWrite</code> below: the app needs one grant per Kaimon tool, and the wildcard <code>mcp(kaimon/*)</code> has no effect there. A Kaimon tool that the template does not name gets no rule, and the app asks for it. Last in <code>allow</code>, the first word of each command of the template&#39;s <code>sandbox.excludedCommands</code>, which Claude Code runs outside its sandbox, becomes a <code>command(&lt;word&gt;)</code> rule, unless the list holds it: <code>command(gh)</code>, <code>command(git)</code>, <code>command(glab)</code> and <code>command(ps)</code>, in the CLI&#39;s settings and in the app&#39;s grants. No <code>unsandboxed(…)</code> rule is written. <code>guard.py</code> applies the template&#39;s Bash denies and asks before these rules, so <code>git push --force</code> and <code>git add -A</code> are still refused, and <code>gh pr merge</code> still asks. The path list of <code>guard.py</code> refuses a write to <code>config/config.json</code> by a tool that writes a file, so an agent cannot widen its own grants with one. A command or a <code>multi_replace_file_content</code> call that writes the file gets <code>ask</code> from the guard, not <code>deny</code>.</p><p>Last in <code>deny</code>, each entry of the template&#39;s <code>sandbox.filesystem.denyWrite</code>, the paths that Claude Code&#39;s sandbox does not let a command write, becomes a <code>read_file(&lt;path&gt;)</code> rule, with <code>~</code> as the home directory, unless the list holds it, when the entry is at or below the research root. An entry outside the research root, such as <code>~/.gitconfig</code>, renders no rule and no warning: the app&#39;s sandbox mounts only the workspace, so a sandboxed command cannot write there anyway. The guard lifts <code>git</code> out of the app&#39;s sandbox, so a <code>.git/hooks</code> or <code>.git/config</code> that a sandboxed command could write would run outside it. The rule is a <code>read_file</code> deny because of a probe in the app. A <code>write_file</code> deny of a path below the workspace hid much more than its path, all of <code>~/Research/.scratch</code> in the probe. A <code>read_file</code> deny of <code>&lt;repo&gt;/.git/hooks</code> and <code>&lt;repo&gt;/.git/config</code> stopped a sandboxed command from reading <code>.git/config</code> and from writing either path, and left the rest of the workspace readable. It also hides the two paths from a sandboxed read. A sandboxed <code>git</code> cannot read <code>~/.gitconfig</code> and fails anyway, and a lifted <code>git</code> runs outside the sandbox; a sandboxed tool that reads <code>.git/config</code> itself, such as a library that finds a repository&#39;s remote, can no longer read it. The app honours no wildcard in a path, so an entry <code>~/Research/**/&lt;rest&gt;</code> becomes one rule <code>&lt;repo&gt;/&lt;rest&gt;</code> for each directory at or below the research root that holds a <code>.git</code> directory, nested repositories included. The walk is the one of <code>harness trust</code>: it skips <code>.git</code>, <code>.scratch</code>, <code>.worktrees</code> and <code>node_modules</code>, and follows no symbolic link. A worktree, whose <code>.git</code> is a file, is covered by its main repository. The rules name each repository by its real path. One warning of the dry run names the number of repositories that these rules cover. A repository cloned after the install is covered after the next install, and the dry run then shows the changed number and <code>config/config.json</code> as a change. Any other entry with a wildcard whose directory before the first wildcard is the research root, above it or below it, gives a warning that names it, and no rule.</p><p>The install exits with status 2, before it writes a file, in these cases among others:</p><ul><li>the CLI&#39;s <code>settings.json</code>, the app&#39;s <code>config.json</code> or <code>mcp_config.json</code> is not a regular file or not a JSON object;</li><li>the app&#39;s <code>userSettings</code> or <code>globalPermissionGrants</code> is not an object, or its <code>allow</code>, <code>deny</code> or <code>ask</code> is not a list;</li><li>two sources hold the same path;</li><li>an instruction file or a rule holds an include <code>@[…](…)</code> or has 24,000 bytes or more, or all of them together reach the budget that Antigravity&#39;s rules share;</li><li><code>models.toml</code> has no <code>[antigravity]</code> table, or the table does not give each tier one of the words <code>inherit</code>, <code>flash</code>, <code>flash_lite</code> and <code>pro</code>;</li><li><code>TOOLS</code> does not map a tool of an agent.</li></ul><p>A council of <code>models.toml</code> gives one warning for each agent that it names, and writes no seat. An installed rule, skill, hook or agent with no source is an EXTRA line.</p><p><code>fixtures/</code> holds the expected frontmatter and &quot;Under Antigravity&quot; section of four rendered agents, which the cases compare.</p><h2 id="antigravity-guard-py" tabindex="-1"><code>antigravity/guard.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#antigravity-guard-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`antigravity/guard.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <code>PreToolUse</code> hook of Antigravity. It gets each tool call and answers with a decision. It runs the five shell guards and the working-tree check of <code>sandbox-semantics.py</code> on <code>run_command</code>, <code>kaimon-session.py</code> on a Kaimon <code>ex</code> or <code>manage_repl</code> call, and the <code>deny</code> list of <code>guard-paths.json</code> on each path argument and each word of a command. A glob in a word, a recursive command, a <code>cd</code>, the <code>Cwd</code> of a call and the <code>SearchPath</code> of <code>grep_search</code> also refuse a directory that holds a path of the list. On <code>run_command</code> it applies the template&#39;s Bash denies and asks, from the lists <code>bash_deny</code> and <code>bash_ask</code>, to each segment of the command, the bodies of <code>$(…)</code>, backticks and <code>( … )</code> included. A rule matches the words of a segment from any word, so an assignment, a wrapper such as <code>env</code> or a keyword such as <code>then</code> before the command does not hide it, and a quoted word is one word. On a tool that writes a file, it also applies the <code>edit</code> list, the template&#39;s Edit denies, and the <code>ask</code> list, its Edit asks. The <code>deny</code> list holds the files of the layer itself. A refusal of a guard becomes <code>deny</code>, an &quot;ask&quot; answer <code>force_ask</code>, and a pass <code>ask</code>, which leaves Antigravity&#39;s own rules in force. It fails closed: a guard that cannot run, fails, prints output that is not a JSON answer or times out gives <code>deny</code>. A <code>run_command</code> that it does not deny, a <code>force_ask</code> included, also gets <code>&quot;overwrite&quot;: {&quot;BypassSandbox&quot;: …}</code>: true only when <code>top_level</code> of <code>sandbox-semantics.py</code> lifts the command and none of the guard&#39;s screens applies, else false; a deny and a call of any other tool carry no <code>overwrite</code>.</p><h2 id="antigravity-antigravity-delta-md" tabindex="-1"><code>antigravity/ANTIGRAVITY-DELTA.md</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#antigravity-antigravity-delta-md" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`antigravity/ANTIGRAVITY-DELTA.md`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The global instruction file of Antigravity. <code>harness install</code> writes it as <code>~/.gemini/config/GEMINI.md</code>, byte for byte, in place of <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>. The core rules, <code>RTK.md</code> and the tree instructions load beside it as always-on rules.</p><p>It states the facts of Antigravity:</p><ul><li>the two controls: the terminal sandbox of <code>agy</code>, with the rules of its <code>settings.json</code>, and the guard hook on every tool call, in the app and in <code>agy</code>;</li><li>the guard hook decides the sandbox of each command, so the agent does not set <code>BypassSandbox</code>;</li><li>in the app, a call of a subagent that needs a grant shows the app&#39;s dialog, as a call of the main agent does;</li><li>Antigravity runs no RTK rewrite, so a command runs as typed;</li><li>there is no worktree hook: make the worktree with <code>git worktree add</code>;</li><li>the <code>Agent</code> tool is <code>invoke_subagent</code>, a subagent replies through <code>send_message</code>, and no agent sets an effort;</li><li>each agent&#39;s model is the Antigravity tier of <code>[antigravity]</code>, and a council has no seat;</li><li>read each <code>CLAUDE.md</code> on the path to the working directory yourself, because Antigravity reads none;</li><li>read a rule of <code>~/.gemini/config/rules/</code> before the work that it names, because none loads by itself;</li><li>the skills are Antigravity&#39;s own copies in <code>~/.gemini/config/skills/</code>;</li><li>there is no session scratchpad.</li></ul><h2 id="antigravity-under-antigravity-md" tabindex="-1"><code>antigravity/UNDER-ANTIGRAVITY.md</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#antigravity-under-antigravity-md" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`antigravity/UNDER-ANTIGRAVITY.md`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The text of the &quot;Under Antigravity&quot; section that the Antigravity adapter puts before the body of an agent. The file has one level-2 section for each mechanism: <code>intro</code>, <code>skills</code>, <code>worktree</code>, <code>agent</code>, <code>background</code>, <code>mcp</code> and <code>pr</code>. <code>render_agent</code> takes <code>intro</code>, then the section of each mechanism that the agent uses: preloaded skills, worktree isolation, the <code>Agent</code> tool, a <code>run_in_background</code> in the body, a Kaimon tool, or a body that says to open a pull request. In <code>skills</code>, <code>{skills}</code> becomes the names of the agent&#39;s skills. An agent that uses none of these mechanisms gets no section. When a section that the adapter needs is missing, the install exits with status 2.</p>',107)])])}const m=o(s,[["render",c]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
